    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-12416",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4179839d-85c4-5325-9293-e332bd209597",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Inverse Function Theorem",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14109277/assets/6e97c59a7c736ec70142bc402566385fc2cd9c6aa78e304d70cefab1027078c1.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=0$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=-3.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem?\n\n- $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n\n- $f^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=0$\n\n- The derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y=-1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}$",
      "worked_solution": "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *$f^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $f$ is not continuous at $x = -3.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement II is also false. Notice that $y = 0 = f(0).$  However, the function $f$ does *not* satisfy the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 0$ since $f'(0) = 0.$ So, the theorem can't be applied in this situation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $y = -1.1 = f(2),$ and the function $f$ satisfies the conditions for the inverse function theorem at $x = 2.$ Indeed,\n\n- $f$ is continuously differentiable on an open set containing $x=2,$ for example, $(0,4),$ and\n\n- the derivative of $f$ is nonzero at $x = 2,$ i.e., $f'(2) \\neq 0.$\n\nTherefore, by the inverse function theorem, the derivative of $f^{-1}(y)$ at $y = -1.1$ equals $\\dfrac{1}{f'(2)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-7127",
      "knowledge_point_id": "bf5e8b36-38f0-5dd9-8b76-82392e593b73",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation",
      "problem": "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14109277/assets/4a20bc6cf78708f70fb1a696b9dc5971feef974df6429fa4169f82881c6d22e0.png)",
      "worked_solution": "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14109277/assets/665b1e0592a0b9d1528b4dedd05eb0410f5b08e70c7b7e1dbd9089b11bf797e6.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the locations of the critical points of $\\mathbf T,$ we need to solve \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}  = 0.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = u^2-v^2$ and $y(u,v) = u+v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-2v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -2v \\\\ \\,\\,1\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(u+v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} =0.$ So, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 2(u+v) & =0 \\\\ u & =-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $T$ consist of those points that lie on the line $u=-v$  in the $uv$-plane.",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-11157",
      "knowledge_point_id": "2fe16334-8f63-56ed-bd58-95e095993b9f",
      "knowledge_point": "Jacobian Determinants of Inverse Transformations",
      "problem": "Consider all non-critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v),y(u,v)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y)\\to(u(x,y), v(x,y))$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14109277/assets/2614cbd879ea1014caf69d1239b69e81ebefcc76b8946962092cb83cc96dc987.png)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14109277/assets/9b0ee6271962e3ede406351f05d805ae60f4b37ce5a45489b117ab3786ed3c23.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ and $v(x,y) = e^{x-y}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x-y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x-y} & -{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x+y}\\cdot (-{e}^{x-y})-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y}-{e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x-y} \\\\  & =-2uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2uv}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
